#: ../../../blog/2017/sensors-support.rst:10
msgid "Sensors: temperatures, battery, CPU frequency"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2017/sensors-support.rst:12
msgid ""
"psutil 5.1.0 is out. This release introduces new APIs to retrieve hardware "
"temperatures, battery status, and CPU frequency information."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2017/sensors-support.rst:16
msgid "Temperatures"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2017/sensors-support.rst:18
msgid ""
"You can now retrieve hardware temperatures (:pr:`962`). This is currently "
"available on Linux only."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2017/sensors-support.rst:21
msgid ""
"On Windows it's hard to do in a hardware-agnostic way. I ran into 3 WMI-"
"based approaches, none of which worked with my hardware, so I gave up."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2017/sensors-support.rst:23
msgid ""
"On macOS it seems relatively easy, but my virtualized macOS box doesn't "
"support sensors, so I gave up for lack of hardware. If someone wants to give "
"it a try, be my guest (:gh:`371`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2017/sensors-support.rst:27
msgid ""
">>> import psutil\n"
">>> psutil.sensors_temperatures()\n"
"{'acpitz': [shwtemp(label='', current=47.0, high=103.0, critical=103.0)],\n"
" 'asus': [shwtemp(label='', current=47.0, high=None, critical=None)],\n"
" 'coretemp': [shwtemp(label='Physical id 0', current=52.0, high=100.0, "
"critical=100.0),\n"
"              shwtemp(label='Core 0', current=45.0, high=100.0, "
"critical=100.0),\n"
"              shwtemp(label='Core 1', current=52.0, high=100.0, "
"critical=100.0),\n"
"              shwtemp(label='Core 2', current=45.0, high=100.0, "
"critical=100.0),\n"
"              shwtemp(label='Core 3', current=47.0, high=100.0, "
"critical=100.0)]}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2017/sensors-support.rst:40
msgid "Battery status"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2017/sensors-support.rst:42
msgid ""
"Battery status information is now available on Linux, Windows and FreeBSD (:"
"pr:`963`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2017/sensors-support.rst:45
msgid ""
">>> import psutil\n"
">>>\n"
">>> def secs2hours(secs):\n"
"...     mm, ss = divmod(secs, 60)\n"
"...     hh, mm = divmod(mm, 60)\n"
"...     return \"%d:%02d:%02d\" % (hh, mm, ss)\n"
"...\n"
">>> battery = psutil.sensors_battery()\n"
">>> battery\n"
"sbattery(percent=93, secsleft=16628, power_plugged=False)\n"
">>> print(\"charge = %s%%, time left = %s\" % (battery.percent, "
"secs2hours(battery.secsleft)))\n"
"charge = 93%, time left = 4:37:08"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2017/sensors-support.rst:61
msgid "CPU frequency"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2017/sensors-support.rst:63
msgid ""
"Available on Linux, Windows and macOS (:pr:`952`). Only Linux reports the "
"real-time value (always changing); other platforms return the nominal "
"\"fixed\" value."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2017/sensors-support.rst:67
msgid ""
">>> import psutil\n"
">>> psutil.cpu_freq()\n"
"scpufreq(current=931.42925, min=800.0, max=3500.0)\n"
">>> psutil.cpu_freq(percpu=True)\n"
"[scpufreq(current=2394.945, min=800.0, max=3500.0),\n"
" scpufreq(current=2236.812, min=800.0, max=3500.0),\n"
" scpufreq(current=1703.609, min=800.0, max=3500.0),\n"
" scpufreq(current=1754.289, min=800.0, max=3500.0)]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2017/sensors-support.rst:79
msgid "What CPU a process is on"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2017/sensors-support.rst:81
msgid ""
"Tells you which CPU a process is currently running on, somewhat related to :"
"meth:`Process.cpu_affinity` (:pr:`954`). It's interesting for visualizing "
"how the OS scheduler keeps evenly reassigning processes across CPUs (see "
"the :src:`scripts/cpu_distribution.py` script)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2017/sensors-support.rst:87
msgid "CPU affinity"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2017/sensors-support.rst:89
msgid "A new shorthand is available to set affinity against all eligible CPUs:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2017/sensors-support.rst:91
msgid "Process().cpu_affinity([])"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2017/sensors-support.rst:95
msgid ""
"This was added because on Linux (:gh:`956`) it is not always possible to set "
"affinity against all CPUs directly. It is equivalent to:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2017/sensors-support.rst:98
msgid "psutil.Process().cpu_affinity(list(range(psutil.cpu_count())))"
msgstr ""

